WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.500
Read the manual before administering a standardized test.

2
00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:08.120
An accommodation changes access while preserving the construct and procedures.

3
00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:13.000
Extra cues forbidden by the manual, translated items, or altered response requirements

4
00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:18.200
can be modifications; the original standard score may no longer be comparable.

5
00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:21.260
Norm referenced means comparison with peers.

6
00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:25.760
Criterion referenced means comparison with an expected performance level.

7
00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:28.440
Chronological age selects the correct age norms.

8
00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:34.460
Adjusted age subtracts prematurity for the developmental comparison specified by the source.

9
00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:38.440
A basal establishes credit for easier items; a ceiling stops

10
00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:41.340
testing as harder items are likely to be missed.

11
00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:44.440
Their exact rules belong to the particular manual.

12
00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:47.760
The raw score is the points earned under those rules.

13
00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:52.260
An incorrect age or raw score can contaminate every derived score.

14
00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:54.460
The course table gives standard scores a mean

15
00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:57.900
of one hundred and standard deviation fifteen.

16
00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:00.300
Scaled scores use ten and three.

17
00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:02.540
A z score uses zero and one.

18
00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:04.440
Stanines use five and two.

19
00:01:04.460 --> 00:01:07.300
Standard score one hundred fifteen and scaled score thirteen

20
00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:10.360
are both one standard deviation above their means.

21
00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:14.080
Percentile rank describes the norm group at or below the score.

22
00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:16.420
It is not percent correct.

23
00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:21.200
An age equivalent is descriptive, not an eligibility or severity rule.

24
00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:23.360
A wider confidence interval accompanies a higher

25
00:01:23.380 --> 00:01:27.280
confidence level; it expresses measurement uncertainty.

26
00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:30.700
Validity asks whether the intended construct is measured.

27
00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:33.940
Reliability asks whether results are consistent.

28
00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:37.320
Standardization asks whether procedures are uniform.

29
00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:39.980
Freedom from bias asks about fairness.

30
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:43.420
Sensitivity detects a disorder among people who have it.

31
00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:46.540
Specificity correctly clears people who do not.

32
00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:49.756
State the convention for boundary scores and age borrowing.
